#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107146 :tutorial/content "Recall that for $x >0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{x}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this to all values $x\\neq 0$ by including an absolute value inside the natural logarithm: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{|x|}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral of the reciprocal function is\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln |x| + C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "83cb4468-abfe-5dfb-9c34-b104314cb2f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 706 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107147 :tutorial/content "For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C = \\ln K,$ where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "87815696-d64e-5c3f-a6e5-025001fb6e95" :tutorial/math-academy-id 709 :tutorial/title "Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}]}
